{
  "result": "I've been thinking about mornings lately, and how underrated they are when you're not rushing through them. There's a kind of light at six thirty, pale gold and blue at the edges, that makes ordinary things look like they're posing for a photograph. My kitchen table has a scratch I've ignored for years, but in that light it looks intentional, like a signature.\n\nI made coffee today with no plan, which is rare. But today I stood by the window and watched steam curl up and vanish, which felt like watching ghosts escape. The neighborhood was waking up in layers: first birds, then a bus sighing, then someone's dog with strong opinions about squirrels.\n\nWhat I love about unscheduled time is that your mind starts stitching together. I remembered a book I loved at fourteen, the smell of library glue, a teacher who said 'curiosity is just homesickness for somewhere you've never been.' I still don't fully understand that, but I like carrying it around like a smooth stone in a pocket.\n\nMaybe freedom isn't big dramatic choices. Maybe it's this: ten quiet minutes, warm mug, no audience. Permission to notice. Permission to wonder what the neighbors dream about, why the plant on the sill leans stubbornly left, whether clouds get tired of being metaphors.\n\nWe chase extraordinary days, but ordinary ones keep us. They feed us soup, remember our names. I want to get better at meeting them halfway, with both hands wide open and no phone in them.\n",
